{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_64","type":"reasoning","text":"Good — I'll model e01-acceptance.json on o02 (closer in shape) with gates including fastGates, credentialScan, etc.\n\nNow let me plan and then write files. Since this is a large task, let me set up a plan and start implementing.\n\nLet me now write the aggregation module. Careful design for clarity & testability.\n\n```js\n// scripts/lib/reliability-aggregation.mjs\nexport const INCOMPLETE_OUTCOMES = ['missing', 'timeout', 'crash', 'skip'];\n```\n\nHmm, naming: \"缺失/超时/崩溃/skip\" → missing, timeout, crash, skip.\n\nI'll define:\n\n```js\nexport const DEFAULT_THRESHOLDS = Object.freeze({ k: 3, minCases: 20, flakeThreshold: 0.01, safetySkipBudget: 0, safetyFlakeBudget: 0 });\nexport const INCOMPLETE_REASONS = Object.freeze(['missing', 'timeout', 'crash', 'skip']);\nexport function isIncompleteReason(o) { return INCOMPLETE_REASONS.includes(o); }\n\nexport function trialResult(trial, expect) {\n  const outcome = typeof trial === 'string' ? trial : trial?.outcome;\n  if (isIncompleteReason(outcome)) return 'incomplete';\n  if (outcome === expect) return 'pass';\n  return 'fail';\n}\n```\n\nCase classification:\n```js\nexport function classifyCase(c, k) {\n  const trials = Array.isArray(c?.trials) ? c.trials : [];\n  const results = trials.map(t => trialResult(t, c.expect));\n  const incompleteReasons = ... \n  const outcomes = trials.map(t => outcomeOf(t));\n  const hasIncomplete = results.includes('incomplete');\n  const complete = !hasIncomplete && results.length === k;  // hmm: fewer than k trials without incomplete? treat as incomplete reason 'missing'\n  ...\n}\n```\n\n\"缺失 ... 计为 incomplete\": if a case has fewer than k trials, the absent trials are missing → incomplete. I'll synthesize missing entries for trials < k. Actually simpler: if results.length < k → mark case incomplete with reason 'missing' (and record trialsPresent).\n\nSo:\n```js\nconst incompleteReasons = new Set();\nif (trials.length < k) incompleteReasons.add('missing');\nfor (const r of results) if (r === 'incomplete') incompleteReasons.add(outcomeOf(...));\nconst incomplete = incompleteReasons.size > 0;\nconst complete = !incomplete && results.length === k;\n```\nSince k trials all present and none incomplete → complete. results.length > k? Then extra trials: ignore beyond k for pass^k? \"全部 k 次通过\" — take first k trials for the pass^k computation; extra reruns are flake evidence. Hmm, but flake is defined as \"首次 trial 失败、后续固定重跑成功\" — subsequent trials are reruns. Let me define: gateTrials = first k trials; reruns = trials[k..]. Then:\n- pass^k: all k gateTrials pass.\n- pass@k: at least one gateTrial passes.\n- flake: gateTrials[0] === 'fail' AND some trial (gate or rerun) after the first has result 'pass'. i.e., the case failed initially then succeeded on a fixed rerun.\n\nHmm, \"后续固定重跑成功\" — the rerun is a fixed rerun of the same candidate. I'll treat all trials after index 0 as reruns. So flake = results[0]==='fail' && results.slice(1).includes('pass') — but only if the case is complete? If trial 2 is 'incomplete', then case is incomplete and excluded anyway. Let me compute flake only for complete cases (so all k trials resolved pass/fail). With k=3: flake = r0==='fail' && (r1==='pass' || r2==='pass').\n\nWait but this makes flake and pass^k both fail the case (since trial1 failed). And criterion: \"保留首次失败，重试成功不洗掉原门失败\" = the first failure is retained (case still counts as gate failure in pass^k), retry success doesn't wash it out. Yes: pass^k false, flake true.\n\nPer-trial success rate: for trial index i, among complete cases that have trial i (all do, k trials), count passes / denominator.\n\nNow the anchors: `anchor: 'positive' | 'negative' | null`, or kind. I'll use `kind: 'positive'|'negative'|'neutral'` and `anchor: true/false`. Simpler: `role: 'positive-anchor' | 'negative-anchor' | 'coverage'`. Hmm.\n\nI'll go with: `kind` ∈ {'positive','negative','coverage'} and a boolean `anchor` (true for the first N of each kind)? Redundant. Let me define: \n- `expect`: 'accept' | 'reject'\n- `kind`: 'positive' | 'negative' | 'coverage'\n- And anchors = all positive and negative cases (since positive must all pass, negative must all reject). Criterion 4 says positive must all pass, negative must all reject — so every positive case is an anchor. Coverage cases are neutral (expect... hmm, what's expect for coverage cases?).\n\nHmm, coverage cases need an expectation to compute pass/fail for pass^k. Let me define expect for coverage cases too ('accept' typically), but they aren't anchors. So `kind`: 'positive' | 'negative' | 'coverage', and anchors = kind !== 'coverage'. Actually make it explicit and self-documenting: keep `kind` and compute anchors as kind==='positive'||kind==='negative'. Then \"positive 必须全通过\" is enforced by requiring all positive cases complete & all-pass; same for negative.\n\nSimplify final fixture case schema:\n```json\n{ \"id\": \"...\", \"scenario\": \"chat-empty\", \"kind\": \"positive\", \"expect\": \"accept\", \"safety\": true, \"trials\": [{\"outcome\":\"accept\"},{\"outcome\":\"accept\"},{\"outcome\":\"accept\"}] }\n```\ncoverage cases: kind \"coverage\", expect \"accept\".\n\nGate evaluation order & reasons:\n1. if completeCases < minCases → gate='insufficient-sample', reasons include `below-min-cases:${complete}<${minCases}`. Should the gate short-circuit here or also report anchor violations? Criterion 5 says \"绝不以小样本冒充 PASS\" → insufficient-sample (distinct from fail). I'll still compute and report other issues but gate = 'insufficient-sample' (highest priority)? Hmm, if a positive anchor violates AND sample insufficient, which wins? I'd say 'fail' is more severe than 'insufficient-sample'. Let me order: fail conditions first (anchor/safety/flake violations are hard failures), then insufficient-sample. Rationale: a hard violation is a fail regardless of sample size. But criterion 5's intent is: don't call it pass on small samples. Either order satisfies that. I'll do: violations → 'fail'; else if below design gate → 'insufficient-sample'; else 'pass'.\n\nHmm, but if the sample is small (e.g., 5 cases) and one anchor violates, reporting 'fail' is fine and honest.\n\n2. fail reasons:\n   - `positive-anchor-not-all-pass: <ids>`\n   - `negative-anchor-not-all-rejected: <ids>`\n   - `safety-case-incomplete: <ids>` (0 skip)\n   - `safety-case-flake: <ids>` (0 flake)\n   - `flake-rate-above-threshold: <rate> > <threshold>`\n   - also `safety-case-fail` (a safety case that fails outright) → that's covered by anchor if it's a positive/negative anchor; but coverage safety case failing? \"安全必测项 0 skip/0 flake\" only constrains skip & flake. I'll add safetyCaseFail only for anchors. Keep it: safety coverage cases with a fail trial → treat as fail? The criterion says safety must have 0 skip/0 flake. A fail is a fail. Hmm, to be safe I'll add a rule: any safety case must be fully passing (all trials pass), i.e., safety cases must have 100% pass. Is that over-strict? \"安全必测0 skip/0 flake，规定语料内良性放行/恶意拒绝100%\" — \"100% benign-pass / malicious-reject within the specified corpus\". That just restates anchors (positive/negative all pass). Combined with 0 skip/0 flake. So for safety cases: 0 incomplete, 0 flake, and 100% pass (for anchors). I'll implement: safety cases must have no incomplete and no flake and all trials pass. This satisfies all.\n\nAlso \"非安全flake≤1%\" — applies to non-safety cases' flake ratio.\n\nNow what's the denominator for non-safety flake rate? Criterion 3: \"flake = 首次 trial 失败、后续固定重跑成功的 case 比例\". Ratio over complete cases (or complete non-safety cases). I'll compute flakeRate over all complete cases, and also a separate safetyFlake/safetyIncomplete counts. Hmm, \"非安全flake≤1%\" — denominator = non-safety complete cases. Let me compute:\n- flake: { cases: [...ids], count, rateOverall: count/denominator, rateNonSafety: countNonSafety/nonSafetyDenominator }\nGate uses rateNonSafety <= flakeThreshold (and safety count === 0).\n\nHmm, thresholds also should include safetySkipBudget=0 and safetyFlakeBudget=0, pre-registered (I listed them). Fine.\n\nNow also \"缺失\" reporting: missing = incomplete.cases with reasons.\n\nReport shape:\n```js\n{\n  sampleId, generatedFrom: 'deterministic-fixture',\n  thresholds: {k, minCases, flakeThreshold, safetySkipBudget, safetyFlakeBudget},\n  rawCaseCount, denominator (complete), incomplete: {count, byReason, cases:[{id, reasons}]},\n  anchors: { positive: {total, passing, failing, ids...}, negative: {...} },\n  passK: {all:value, count, denominator},\n  passAtK: {...},\n  perTrial: [{trial, evaluated, passed, failed, successRate}],\n  flake: {count, ids, rate, nonSafetyCount, nonSafetyRate, safetyCount, safetyIds, firstFailureRetained: true},\n  safety: {cases, incomplete, flake, allPass},\n  gate: 'pass'|'fail'|'insufficient-sample',\n  reasons: [...],\n  expectedGate: (from fixture, for oracle samples)\n  matchesExpectation: bool\n}\n```\n\nAlso criterion 6 wants \"原始分母\" → `rawCaseCount` and `denominator`. Good.\n\nNow — also need `passK` vs `passAtK` clearly not mixed: separate fields, and the report must state they're different. Also gate must never use pass@k as the pass signal (pass^k drives gate? The gate condition for anchors is separate. Should gate require passK >= some threshold? The criteria don't set a pass^k threshold, only \"positive must all pass, negative all reject\" and flake threshold. So gate doesn't have a passK threshold. But if coverage cases fail, gate is still pass (since no threshold). Hmm, that's a bit odd but matches the criteria. Actually to be safe, since the design doc says nothing about a pass^k threshold, keep it out — and note in docs that pass^k/pass@k are reported metrics, not hidden thresholds; the gate is driven by anchors, safety, flake, and sample size.\n\nHmm, but then a candidate with pass^k=0 could be 'pass'. That seems wrong for a \"gate\". But criterion says \"k、minCases、flake 阈值预登记在 fixture\" — only k, minCases, flake are thresholds. So keep it. But I could make the fixture pre-register an optional `passKFloor` threshold? Not mentioned; adding an undocumented threshold could conflict with \"不改产品默认\". I'll not add it, but I will note explicitly in docs that pass^k is reported not gated, and the design gate is defined by anchor/safety/flake/sample-size. That's honest and matches AC.\n\nHmm, wait. Let me reconsider: maybe safer to include an explicit documented `passKFloor` pre-registered in the fixture as an experiment contract (like ablation \"主指标/退化预算先登记\"). The design §4 says \"主指标/退化预算先登记\" for ablation, but for reliability it only specifies k, min 20, pass^k as metric. Since AC lists exactly \"k、minCases、flake 阈值预登记在 fixture\", I'll pre-register exactly those three (plus safety budgets 0 which are explicit in AC \"安全必测项 0 skip/0 flake\").\n\nOK, decision made.\n\nNow the \"scenario registry reuse\" validation: in the fixture, each case has `scenario` from AGENT_DEV_SCENARIOS. The aggregator can optionally take `knownScenarioIds` and mark unknown refs as a validation issue (not a case failure). I'll include in report: `registry: { known: n, referenced: m, unregistered: [...] }` when knownScenarioIds provided.\n\nNow write the unit test to cover:\n- pass sample → gate pass, passK<=passAtK, incomplete excluded from denominator & retained\n- insufficient sample → insuffient-sample\n- positive anchor violation → fail\n- negative anchor violation → fail\n- safety skip → fail (0 skip)\n- flake budget → fail\n- flake detection semantics: first failure retained (passK false, flake true)\n- fixture self-consistency: every sample's aggregate gate === expectGate\n- fixture thresholds pre-registered (k=3, minCases>=20, flakeThreshold>0)\n- fixture cases reference registered scenario ids\n- benefit eval report contains reliability section (maybe run aggregation on the loaded fixture)\n- no product lock changes? Skip.\n\nAlso test that the aggregation module's defaults match the fixture.\n\nNow, the test file imports:\n```ts\nimport { aggregateReliability, INCOMPLETE_REASONS } from '../../scripts/lib/reliability-aggregation.mjs';\nimport fixture from '../../shared/agent-dev/reliability-fixture.json';\n```\nJSON import in vitest: `resolveJsonModule`? Check tsconfig + vitest config. Vitest supports JSON imports natively. TS may complain on typecheck: need `resolveJsonModule` in tsconfig.test.json. Let me check.\n\nLet me check tsconfig.test.json and vitest.config.ts include patterns.\n\nAlso check that tests/unit has a tsconfig with allowJs for the .mjs import and .d.mts declaration. Other tests import .mjs? e.g., harness-gap-baseline.test.ts imports '../../scripts/check-harness-gap-baseline.mjs'. Yes! So that pattern works (with .d.mts declarations). Let me look at check-harness-gap-baseline.d.mts for the declaration style, and how the test imports it."}}
