t me write the test with `// @vitest-environment node`.\n\nCareful with types: importing from '../../scripts/lib/reliability-aggregation.mjs' uses the .d.mts. My test constructs case objects with `kind: 'positive'` — TS needs the literal type; I'll type arrays as `ReliabilityCaseInput[]` by importing types? Importing types from a .d.mts: `import type { ReliabilityCaseInput } from '../../scripts/lib/reliability-aggregation.mjs'`? Hmm, type-only import from the .mjs path resolves the d.mts — should work.\n\nSafer: define local fixture data as plain objects and pass them; TS will check against the declared input type (structural). Use `as const`? If I write inline object literals passed directly to aggregateReliability({cases: [...]}), TS infers and checks. Since kind: 'positive' is a string literal in an object literal context typed as ReliabilityCaseKind, it's fine.\n\nFor arrays built with helper functions, I'll annotate: `const cases: ReliabilityCaseInput[] = [...]`. Let me import the type.\n\nRisk: TS may complain about unused import etc. (noUnusedLocals false in test config).\n\nAlso test file uses `expect(...)`.\n\nLet me be careful that `readFileSync(new URL(...))`? Other tests use `readFileSync('shared/...')` relative to cwd (vitest runs from repo root). harness-gap-baseline.test.ts uses `readFileSync` — let me check how it resolves paths. It imports `readFileSync` and presumably uses relative paths from cwd. Let me check a snippet for the root resolution approach.\n\nFrom the earlier output of harness-gap-baseline.test.ts, I saw `const inventory = ...` at top but truncated. Let me check the first ~20 lines of that file for how it loads JSON."}}
