{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_188","type":"reasoning","text":"Good. Now some final quality checks before wrapping up:\n\n1. Review the test file once for correctness/legibility (it was hand-written; ensure the assertions match what I verified). I verified the key numbers with the replay script, but the test file may contain expectations NOT covered by the replay (e.g., the `classifyCase` describe blocks, `trialResult` mapping, `INCOMPLETE_OUTCOMES`, `'grid'`). Let me re-read the test file fully and double-check each assertion against the implementation mentally / with a quick script.\n\nSpecifically:\n- `trialResult({ outcome: 'timeout' }, 'accept')` → outcomeOf handles object → 'timeout' → incomplete ✓\n- classifyCase({id:'short', expect:'accept', trials:['accept','accept']}, 3) → incompleteReasons ['missing'] ✓ complete false ✓\n- classifyCase({id:'crash', ...trials ['accept','crash','accept']}) → incompleteReasons ['crash'] ✓ (length 3, no missing) ✓\n- rerunAfterPass: ['accept','reject','accept','accept'] → complete true (no incomplete, length 4 ≥3), gateResults 3 → allPass false, anyPass true, flake false ✓\n- rerunAfterFail: ['reject','accept','accept'] → complete, allPass false, anyPass true (has accept), flake true, firstTrialFailed true ✓\n- thresholds throws ✓\n- 'does not collapse': passK {count:2,value:0.5} ✓ passAtK {count:4,value:1} ✓ perTrial [1,0.5,0.75] ✓\n- incomplete describe: raw 26, denominator 22, byReason, ids, gate pass ✓ (verified)\n- flake describe: denominator 23, flake {count 1, cases ['flake-1'], firstFailureRetained true}, nonSafetyCount 1, passK.count 22, gate fail, reasons match ✓\n- late-fail: flake.count 0, gate pass ✓\n- positive anchor: gate fail, anchors.positive.violating ['pos-broken'], reasons match ✓\n- negative anchor: ✓\n- safety skip: denominator >= 20, safety {zeroSkip:false, incomplete:['safety-skip']}, gate fail ✓\n- safety flake: safety {zeroFlake:false, flake:['safety-flake']}, gate fail ✓\n- insufficient: healthy(minCases-3)=17 + 2 = 19 → gate insufficient-sample, reasons match ✓ (verified)\n- empty ✓\n- registry: known = registeredScenarioIds.length, referenced 2, unregistered ['not-a-scenario'] ✓\n- fixture tests ✓\n- exercise all four incomplete reasons: union across samples = missing/timeout/crash/skip ✓\n- benefit eval wiring strings: contains \"from './lib/reliability-aggregation.mjs'\" ✓ (import line is `import { aggregateReliability } from './lib/reliability-aggregation.mjs';` → contains \"from './lib/reliability-aggregation.mjs'\" ✓), contains 'shared/agent-dev/reliability-fixture.json' ✓ (RELIABILITY_FIXTURE const), contains 'report.reliability = buildReliability(' ✓.\n\nOne more: `expect(report.perTrial.map((trial) => trial.successRate)).toEqual([1, 0.5, 0.75]);` ✓ verified.\n\n2. TypeScript type-compat concerns for the test file (typecheck:test would run in a real env):\n   - `import type { ReliabilityCaseInput } from '../../scripts/lib/reliability-aggregation.mjs';` — resolves to the .d.mts? TS resolves './x.mjs' → './x.d.mts'. Yes, that's the documented behavior (`.mjs` → `.d.mts`). ✓\n   - `fixture.samples` typed as any (JSON.parse returns any) → I cast via `as Array<{...}>` ✓\n   - `coverage(id)` returns ReliabilityCaseInput; `{ ...coverage('unknown'), scenario: 'not-a-scenario' }` → fine.\n   - `expect(report.safety).toMatchObject({...})` fine.\n   - `check` helper functions unused? No such helper in the test file (that was the temp script). The test file defines accept3/reject3/positive/negative/coverage/healthy — all used? `accept3` and `reject3` used as default params ✓. `positive` used ✓. `negative` used ✓. `coverage` used ✓. `healthy` used ✓. `registeredScenarioIds` used ✓. `benefitEvalSource` used ✓. `classifyCase` imported and used in the last fixture test ✓. `DEFAULT_THRESHOLDS` used ✓. `GATE_FAIL`, `GATE_INSUFFICIENT_SAMPLE`, `GATE_PASS` used ✓. `INCOMPLETE_OUTCOMES` used ✓. `normalizeThresholds` used ✓. `trialResult` used ✓. `aggregateReliability` used ✓. Good (noUnusedLocals is false anyway).\n   \n   - `report.registry?.unregistered` — registry may be null; using optional chaining ✓. But in the \"keeps the primary sample green\" test I call `expect(pr.registry?.unregistered).toEqual([])` where pr has registry non-null. Fine.\n\n   Hmm, `ReliabilityCaseInput[]` requires `trials?: Array<string | ReliabilityTrialInput>` and my fixture cases come from JSON (any) so no type issue.\n\n   One risk: `expect(fixture.thresholds).toMatchObject({...})` fine.\n   \n   Another: `expect(registry).toMatchObject({ known: registeredScenarioIds.length, referenced: 2, unregistered: ['not-a-scenario'] })` — toMatchObject with extra fields (referencedIds) is fine.\n\n3. ESLint on the test file: rules include @typescript-eslint/no-explicit-any (warn) — we don't use any explicitly. Fine. `@typescript-eslint/no-unused-vars` — all used. Fine.\n\n4. The .d.mts: `export default aggregateReliability;` — declaring default export referencing a function declared later is fine in a d.mts.\n\nHmm, one thing: in the .d.mts I declared `export function normalizeThresholds(thresholds?, fallback?)` etc. and `export default aggregateReliability;`. TS might complain in a declaration file about the default export referencing a function declared above — that's fine.\n\nBut wait: is `scripts/lib/reliability-aggregation.d.mts` included in any tsconfig project? tsconfig.test.json includes \"tests\"; the test imports the .mjs → TS pulls in the .d.mts. Fine.\n\n5. Potential issue: tsconfig.test.json's `include: [\"tests\", ...]` and moduleResolution \"bundler\" with allowImportingTsExtensions — importing '../../scripts/lib/reliability-aggregation.mjs' from a test: TS resolves the .d.mts. Other tests do the same for check-harness-gap-baseline.mjs. ✓\n\n6. `scripts/lib/reliability-aggregation.mjs` is not under any tsconfig include, so no type checking of the .mjs itself. Fine.\n\nNow, let me consider whether I should ALSO verify that the module'